=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: switch0
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The Linux software bridge `br0` on **switch0** — the Layer-2 forwarding device that sits between router0 and the IT-Department LAN (`lan_it`).

**Mechanism:** switch0 bridges its `eth0` port (on segment `link_r0_sw0`, shared with `router0.eth0`) and its `eth1` port (on `lan_it`, shared with all five IT-dept hosts). When `br0` is DOWN, the switch can forward zero frames between those two segments. Every IT-dept host is effectively on an isolated L2 island and cannot ARP-resolve or reach their default gateway 192.168.1.1 (router0's eth0), which explains why the entire department lost the network simultaneously.

**Localization:** `switch0 / br0`. Both member interfaces (`eth0`, `eth1`) are UP and correctly enslaved (`<BROADCAST,MULTICAST,UP,LOWER_UP> master br0`). The bridge flags register `0x1002` — `IFF_BROADCAST | IFF_MULTICAST` only; `IFF_UP` is absent. `operstate=down`, `carrier=0`. `carrier_changes=3` confirms the bridge came up briefly after the startup script ran, then was forced back down.

**Root cause:** The `br0` bridge on switch0 was administratively shut down (likely via an injected `ip link set dev br0 down` running after the startup script). The startup script correctly issues `ip link set dev br0 up`, but that effect was reversed externally. router0 is completely healthy and is not the fault — other zones that route through router0 are fully reachable.

**Fix:** On switch0, run `ip link set dev br0 up`. This restores bridging between the router0 uplink and the IT-dept LAN, immediately re-establishing connectivity for all 192.168.1.x hosts.
=== END ===